"""Original editorial introductions for the Assyria foundation edition."""
ARTICLES = []
def add(slug, title, category, summary, sections, sources, related=(), image=None, note=None):
    ARTICLES.append(dict(slug=slug,title=title,category=category,summary=summary,sections=[dict(title=t,text=p) for t,p in sections],sources=sources,related=list(related),image=image,note=note))

add('assyrian-identity','Understanding Assyrian identity','People & diaspora',
'A starting point for understanding a people through language, memory, community and the names people use for themselves.',[
('A living identity','This archive centres Assyrian history and living culture. Its scope includes ancient Assyria, Aramaic-speaking communities, Syriac literary heritage and modern people who identify as Assyrian. These subjects overlap, but each needs its own historical context. A palace inscription, a medieval hymn and a modern migration interview answer different kinds of questions.'),
('Names deserve care','Assyrian, Chaldean, Syriac and Aramean appear in different ethnic, linguistic and ecclesiastical settings. Individuals and institutions do not all use these names in the same way. The archive follows the self-description of a person or organisation when discussing them. A church name alone cannot establish an individual\'s ethnic identification. Likewise, the fact that someone speaks an Aramaic variety does not automatically make them Assyrian.'),
('How to read the evidence','For events recorded in Scripture, begin with the biblical account and cite its book, chapter and verse. Archaeological and textual scholarship supplies additional historical evidence and context. For language, use specialist linguistic descriptions and recordings. For modern life, add oral histories and community institutions. Sophia Kanna\'s Australian migration account, for example, records one Assyrian family experience; it is valuable testimony without being a census of the whole diaspora. This combination gives a richer account than making one type of evidence stand for every period.'),
('Questions still to develop','The foundation edition does not settle debates about the historical development of modern national consciousness or every competing interpretation of continuity. Those require dedicated articles, named scholarly positions and a wider bibliography. Future contributions should state the period, place, community and source behind each claim.')],
['modern-kanna','modern-aaa','modern-chaldean','gedsh-aramaic'],['australia','faith-and-churches','aramaic'],note='Editorial overview. Self-designations vary; this article does not impose one identity on all related communities.')

add('ancient-assyria','Ancient Assyria','History & archaeology',
'From a city on the Tigris to a major imperial power: an introduction to the places, periods and evidence.',[
('Begin with Ashur','Assyria developed in northern Mesopotamia, with the city of Ashur as a central religious and political reference point. Its history covers changing institutions and territories over many centuries. The Old, Middle and Neo-Assyrian labels are scholarly period divisions; they should not suggest that the same imperial boundaries existed throughout.'),
('Trade before the great palaces','Old Assyrian merchants operating in Anatolia in the early second millennium BCE left written accounts of trade, credit, travel and family affairs. Their tablets illuminate the practical work behind long-distance exchange. They offer a useful counterweight to the much later royal monuments that dominate popular images of Assyria.'),
('The imperial centuries','Assyria expanded in the Middle Assyrian period and again in the ninth to seventh centuries BCE. Rulers constructed major capitals at Kalhu, Dur-Sharrukin and Nineveh. Monumental art proclaimed royal authority, while inscriptions presented campaigns and building works in the king\'s preferred terms. These are important primary sources, but their political purpose matters when assessing what they tell us.'),
('After the empire','The late seventh-century BCE collapse of Assyrian imperial power marks a political transformation. It should not be treated as proof that every local population or cultural practice disappeared. Ashur itself experienced later occupation, including a Parthian revival. Studying subsequent centuries requires their own evidence rather than filling the interval with assumptions.'),
('A route through the archive','Read the city entries for geography, the trade article for everyday documents, and the art entries for visual interpretation. Keep a distinction between an artefact\'s ancient setting, its modern excavation and its later museum history. The full story includes all three.')],
['met-assyria','met-caravan','unesco-ashur','met-court'],['ashur','old-assyrian-trade','nimrud','nineveh'],image='relief',note='Introductory synthesis. Ancient chronology can vary between scholarly systems; dates are approximate where indicated.')

add('ashur','Ashur: city, sanctuary and crossroads','Places & heritage',
'The Tigris city at the heart of Assyrian political and religious life.',[
('On the Tigris','Ashur, also written Assur or Aššur, is the archaeological site known as Qal\'at Sherqat in present-day Iraq. UNESCO dates the city to the third millennium BCE and describes its setting near the transition between rain-fed and irrigated agriculture.'),
('More than a capital','Ashur was an important trading centre and the religious centre associated with the god Ashur. A city\'s religious significance could endure after a ruler moved the court elsewhere. The city therefore belongs in histories of commerce and ritual as well as accounts of kingship.'),
('A site with several lives','The site\'s history extends beyond the Assyrian imperial period. UNESCO records a revival under the Parthians. Archaeological layers must be dated and interpreted individually: a later building cannot automatically be read as evidence of an unchanged earlier institution. For conservation information, consult UNESCO\'s dated reports rather than assuming the site\'s condition is static.')],
['unesco-ashur'],['ancient-assyria','old-assyrian-trade','heritage-stewardship'])

add('old-assyrian-trade','Merchants, caravans and clay tablets','History & archaeology',
'Commercial records open a window onto travel, enterprise and family life in the Old Assyrian world.',[
('A trading network','In the early second millennium BCE, merchants from Ashur established settlements in Anatolia. Tin and textiles travelled through these networks in exchange for precious metals. Surviving tablets record investments, expenditure, loans and disputes as well as correspondence.'),
('Read a real document','The Met\'s caravan account, object 66.245.10, is a concrete starting point. Its record connects an individual clay object to the logistics of moving goods. A catalogue description is useful, but a full historical argument also requires a text edition, translation and the tablet\'s archaeological context.'),
('A different Assyrian world','These commercial archives predate the great Neo-Assyrian palace reliefs by many centuries. Reading them together is rewarding precisely because their social settings differ. The archive will expand this topic through individual documents, named merchants and the roles of women in family correspondence, using specialist editions.')],
['met-caravan'],['akkadian-and-cuneiform','ancient-assyria','research-guide'])

add('nimrud','Nimrud, ancient Kalhu','Places & heritage',
'A royal capital whose architecture and sculpture shaped the visual language of Assyrian kingship.',[
('A capital rebuilt','Ashurnasirpal II, who reigned approximately 883-859 BCE, made Kalhu a major royal capital. The site is widely known today as Nimrud. The palace sculptures now held in museums originally formed parts of architectural spaces, rather than independent pictures.'),
('Reconstructing a setting','The Met\'s Assyrian Sculpture Court evokes a palace setting while explicitly acknowledging that it is not a full reconstruction. Its reliefs originated in multiple rooms. That distinction helps visitors separate a modern display from a securely known ancient arrangement.'),
('Look closely','Observe changes in scale, repeated figures, patterned clothing and the relationship between carved imagery and inscriptions. Then ask where a panel stood and who could see it. Those contextual questions are as valuable as identifying the king or protective figure.')],
['met-court','met-lion'],['lamassu','royal-reliefs','ancient-assyria'],image='relief')

add('khorsabad','Dur-Sharrukin and Khorsabad','Places & heritage',
'Sargon II\'s planned royal city, known through its remains, excavations and monumental guardians.',[
('A royal foundation','Dur-Sharrukin, at modern Khorsabad, was built as a capital by Sargon II. It was a short-lived centre of royal power. The French Ministry of Culture\'s archaeological resource introduces the site, its palace and its excavation history.'),
('Ancient city, modern discoveries','Paul-Emile Botta began major excavations at Khorsabad in 1843. Excavation records are part of the evidence, but nineteenth-century discovery narratives should be read alongside the history of the place and its local communities.'),
('What needs further study','A complete entry should distinguish the original city plan, surviving archaeology, reconstructed architecture and objects dispersed among collections. Museum photographs alone cannot establish a palace\'s complete appearance. This introduction links to specialist material rather than presenting an imagined reconstruction as fact.')],
['khorsabad-ministry'],['nimrud','lamassu','heritage-stewardship'])

add('nineveh','Nineveh and the library of Ashurbanipal','History & archaeology',
'Royal collections of clay tablets reveal a wider world of scholarship, copying and commentary.',[
('A city of collected knowledge','Nineveh housed the royal scholarly collections associated with Ashurbanipal. Their tablets are central to the study of ancient Mesopotamian learning. The phrase "library of Ashurbanipal" is useful, but should not imply a modern public lending library.'),
('Part of a larger network','Yale\'s Cuneiform Commentaries Project distinguishes temple, palace and private collections across several Mesopotamian cities. Nineveh\'s collections belonged within this wider scholarly landscape. Calling them the world\'s first library would erase earlier and parallel traditions.'),
('Reading tablets today','Researchers compare manuscripts, identify joins between fragments and interpret texts with attention to copying and commentary. CDLI offers catalogue and text resources across cuneiform cultures. A digital record is a route into the evidence, rather than permission to reproduce every photograph or translation associated with it.')],
['yale-libraries','cdli'],['akkadian-and-cuneiform','research-guide','ancient-assyria'])

add('lamassu','Lamassu: guardians in stone','Arts & traditions',
'Human heads, powerful bodies and wings: the monumental figures at Assyrian palace thresholds.',[
('Protection and presence','Assyrian lamassu combined a human head with a winged bull or lion body. They stood at important palace entrances as protective figures. The Met\'s human-headed winged lion from Nimrud dates to approximately 883-859 BCE and is carved from gypsum alabaster.'),
('Why five legs?','Some monumental examples have five legs. Seen from the front they appear firmly planted; seen from the side they seem to stride. This solution responds to a visitor moving past a doorway. It is a useful reminder to read monumental sculpture through viewpoint and architecture.'),
('Ancient object, contemporary meaning','A lamassu may now function as a cultural emblem, an artistic reference or an image of heritage. Those contemporary uses deserve documentation in their own right. They should be distinguished from what an individual ancient sculpture\'s context supports. The photographs in this archive identify the actual museum object and its licence.')],
['met-lion','met-lamassu'],['nimrud','royal-reliefs','heritage-stewardship'],image='lamassu')

add('royal-reliefs','Reading Assyrian palace art','Arts & traditions',
'Reliefs preserve extraordinary craftsmanship and carefully constructed statements about power.',[
('Art within architecture','Assyrian stone panels formed parts of palace interiors. Their scale, arrangement and proximity to thresholds affected how they were experienced. Museum installations can help evoke this relationship, while remaining modern interpretations.'),
('Ask who is speaking','Royal imagery is not a neutral photographic record. A ruler commissioned images to represent authority, ritual or military success. Read scenes alongside inscriptions, archaeological context and independent evidence before drawing conclusions about a particular event.'),
('A practical looking guide','Begin with material and object provenance. Note the figure\'s posture, dress, gestures and repeated motifs. Check which elements a museum identifies confidently and which remain interpretations. Further articles should explore individual works, workshop practices, colour and the histories of excavation and collecting.')],
['met-court','met-assyria','met-relief'],['nimrud','lamassu','research-guide'],image='relief')

add('akkadian-and-cuneiform','Akkadian and cuneiform','Language & literature',
'A language and a writing system are different things. This distinction unlocks the ancient evidence.',[
('Language versus script','Akkadian is an ancient Semitic language; Assyrian and Babylonian are conventionally treated as its varieties. Cuneiform is a writing system used for more than one language. A tablet written with wedge-shaped signs is therefore not automatically an Assyrian text.'),
('Finding texts','CDLI provides records, images and textual resources for cuneiform inscriptions across periods and languages. Search by object, collection and linguistic attribution where available. A transliteration represents the signs in scholarly notation; a translation interprets their meaning in another language.'),
('The modern-language distinction','Modern Assyrian Neo-Aramaic belongs within Aramaic. It should not be described as present-day spoken Akkadian. Contact, historical memory and cultural inheritance are different questions from a language\'s linguistic classification. Follow the Aramaic and Sureth articles for the living-language side of the story.')],
['cambridge-mesopotamian-languages','cdli','gedsh-aramaic'],['aramaic','old-assyrian-trade','nineveh'])

add('aramaic','Aramaic: a family of histories','Language & literature',
'A broad linguistic tradition with many historical forms and modern speaker communities.',[
('One label, many varieties','Aramaic is a Northwest Semitic language tradition with a long and varied history. The name covers forms used in different regions, periods and communities. It does not designate one unchanged language shared identically by every speaker across time.'),
('Syriac and living speech','Classical Syriac is an important literary and liturgical Aramaic variety. Modern Neo-Aramaic includes several distinct groups and local varieties. Cambridge\'s NENA project documents North-Eastern varieties, including those historically spoken by Christian and Jewish communities. Surayt, associated with Tur Abdin, needs its own description.'),
('Choose a learning route','Before selecting a course, identify the goal: reading Classical Syriac, speaking a family\'s local variety or studying a particular regional standard. Record the teacher\'s terminology and pronunciation tradition. Materials aimed at one goal may be valuable without being interchangeable with materials for another.'),
('What the archive will preserve','Future language entries should pair writing with native-speaker recordings, named local varieties and a clear transliteration key. The foundation edition links to documented external resources. It does not generate unreviewed pronunciation audio or claim that a short word list represents every community.')],
['gedsh-aramaic','gedsh-syriac','cambridge-nena','gedsh-turoyo'],['classical-syriac','sureth','surayt','endangered-languages'])

add('classical-syriac','Classical Syriac','Language & literature',
'The literary and liturgical language of a wide intellectual and religious heritage.',[
('Origins and reach','Syriac is an Aramaic variety associated in its origins with Edessa, modern Urfa. It developed a large literary tradition, including prose, poetry and translations. Its texts preserve much more than ecclesiastical history: they also inform political, intellectual and cultural research.'),
('A literary tradition','The fourth to seventh centuries were especially productive in Syriac literature. Manuscripts and later copies transmit works across many periods. The date of a physical manuscript and the date of the text it contains may be centuries apart.'),
('Reading today','Classical Syriac continues in literary and liturgical use alongside living Neo-Aramaic varieties. Learning to read it requires attention to grammar, vocalisation and script tradition. The manuscript and script guides point to specialist teaching resources; the sources remain the place for fuller linguistic description.')],
['gedsh-syriac','syriaca-intro'],['syriac-scripts','manuscripts','ephrem'])

add('sureth','Sureth and North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic','Language & literature',
'Living speech, regional variation and literary practice across related communities.',[
('A name with local meanings','Sureth is used for several North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic varieties, especially in northern Iraq. Its exact scope varies between speakers and publications. A responsible word list should say whose variety it records rather than presenting one form as universal.'),
('Written and spoken heritage','The GEDSH entry describes literary use attested from the late sixteenth century and discusses religious poems and recorded wedding songs. The history of writing is one part of the picture; everyday speech and oral performance require their own records.'),
('Listen to documented voices','Cambridge\'s NENA project offers recorded material and descriptions organised by dialect. Such resources help readers encounter actual variation. When comparing vocabulary, preserve the locality, speaker context and transcription system supplied by the documentation project.')],
['gedsh-sureth','cambridge-nena'],['aramaic','endangered-languages','oral-history'])

add('surayt','Surayt, also known as Turoyo','Language & literature',
'A modern Aramaic tradition associated with Tur Abdin and its diaspora.',[
('Place and language','Surayt, often called Turoyo in scholarship, is associated with Tur Abdin. It has its own linguistic and literary history and should not be reduced to a generic version of Sureth.'),
('A growing written life','The GEDSH account describes wider written use in the second half of the twentieth century, including diaspora publications and writing for children. Literary development and spoken tradition belong together; a relatively recent print history does not make a spoken language new.'),
('A course to explore','Freie Universitat Berlin announced the advanced SLOMO SURAYT course in 2020. Its online learning materials include grammar, texts and a glossary. Follow the university\'s project link to the course and review its terms before reproducing any exercises. Free learning access is different from an unrestricted content licence.')],
['gedsh-turoyo','fu-surayt'],['aramaic','syriac-scripts','endangered-languages'])

add('syriac-scripts','Syriac scripts and the written word','Language & literature',
'Estrangela, Serto and East Syriac are writing traditions with histories of use and adaptation.',[
('Recognising the traditions','HMML School introduces Estrangela, Serto and East Syriac through manuscript examples. Letter forms, vowel marks and scribal practices vary. Script labels describe writing traditions; they do not provide a rigid test of ethnicity.'),
('A script can cross languages','Syriac script has also been used for non-Aramaic languages. Garshuni, in the teaching resource linked here, describes Arabic written using Syriac letters. This is another reason to keep script and language separate.'),
('Reading responsibly','A beautiful letterform is not enough to establish a correct transcription. Check orientation, joining, vowel marks and the manuscript\'s hand. Future alphabet lessons in this archive will need a stated tradition and specialist review. For now, HMML\'s palaeography lessons provide a stronger foundation than an unattributed alphabet graphic.')],
['hmml-palaeography'],['classical-syriac','manuscripts','surayt'])

add('manuscripts','Manuscripts and digital collections','Language & literature',
'A guide to finding texts, understanding catalogue records and respecting collection rights.',[
('More than religious books','Syriac manuscript collections include scripture and hymns alongside grammar, dictionaries, philosophy and natural history. The John Rylands collection at Manchester offers an institutional starting point, with holdings spanning many centuries.'),
('Distributed heritage','HMML\'s Voobus collection documents manuscripts from several countries. The syri.ac discovery guide points to digitised material held across institutions. No single portal contains every surviving manuscript, and a catalogue record does not mean that every page is digitised or openly downloadable.'),
('How to cite a manuscript','Record the holding institution, collection, shelfmark, folio or page, catalogue URL and access date. Distinguish the manuscript\'s copying date from the composition date of its contents. Check image rights independently from the copyright status of the ancient text.'),
('Books, archives and modern scholarship','eBethArke, developed by Beth Mardutho and Rutgers, brings together printed books, manuscripts, recordings and other resources. Its range is a reminder that digital preservation covers modern intellectual life as well as ancient and medieval material.')],
['manchester-syriac','hmml-voobus','syriac-digimss','rutgers-ebetharke'],['classical-syriac','syriac-scripts','research-guide'])

add('ephrem','Ephrem: poetry and theology','People & diaspora',
'A fourth-century writer whose hymns remain central to the study of Syriac literature.',[
('A documented literary figure','Ephrem, who died in 373 CE, was a deacon, poet and theologian associated with Nisibis and later Edessa. His works include hymns, metrical homilies, prose and biblical commentary. The scholarly GEDSH entry is the starting point used here.'),
('Read the works with care','Later authors attributed additional works to Ephrem, and later biographical traditions are not all reliable evidence for his life. An article about him should distinguish securely attributed writing from uncertain or pseudonymous material.'),
('Where to go next','A fuller treatment can examine an individual hymn through an identified edition and translation, then place its imagery within its theological and literary setting. Modern translations have their own copyright. This introduction links to scholarship rather than reproducing a complete translated hymn.')],
['gedsh-ephrem'],['classical-syriac','manuscripts','faith-and-churches'])

add('faith-and-churches','Faith and the Syriac Christian traditions','Faith & communities',
'A guide to related churches, shared literary inheritances and distinct institutional histories.',[
('A shared field, distinct churches','Assyrian cultural history overlaps with several Syriac Christian traditions. The Assyrian Church of the East, Chaldean Catholic Church and Syriac Orthodox Church have distinct institutional identities. This introductory selection is not a complete directory of every church or every community within the archive\'s scope.'),
('Tradition and historical evidence','Churches may describe their origins through apostolic tradition. That is important evidence of ecclesiastical self-understanding. It should be identified as tradition when it is different from the earliest surviving written documentation. The Assyrian Church\'s Australian account itself distinguishes its traditional foundation from written records traced to the late second century.'),
('Ethnicity is a separate question','Religious affiliation does not determine every member\'s ethnic identification. Use individuals\' preferred names and avoid turning a church history into a verdict on all related identities. Equally, people can participate in Assyrian cultural life without sharing one denomination or religious practice.'),
('How the archive will expand','Future church entries should separate doctrine, liturgy, architecture, institutional chronology and local parish life. Official sources are valuable for self-description; comparative scholarship is necessary for contested historical interpretation. Clergy directories, service times and leadership information need current verification before publication.')],
['modern-acoe','modern-chaldean','modern-syriac-constitution','gedsh-syriac'],['church-of-the-east','chaldean-church','syriac-orthodox','assyrian-identity'])

add('church-of-the-east','The Assyrian Church of the East','Faith & communities',
'An introduction through the church\'s own account of its historical and Syriac heritage.',[
('A first-party account','The Australian church\'s historical overview describes development under Parthian and Persian rule and identifies Syriac as its traditional ecclesiastical language. Its story belongs within a wider history of Christianity east of the Roman frontier.'),
('Foundations and evidence','The church traces its foundation to apostolic activity while noting written records from the late second century. The archive preserves that distinction rather than presenting a traditional first-century date as an independently demonstrated event.'),
('Further coverage','A full treatment needs scholarship on theology, liturgy, missions, institutions and modern history. The foundation edition avoids time-sensitive claims about leadership or parish schedules. Follow the official church source for its own descriptions and current institutional information.')],
['modern-acoe'],['faith-and-churches','chaldean-church','classical-syriac'])

add('chaldean-church','The Chaldean Catholic Church','Faith & communities',
'A distinct Catholic church within the wider heritage of the Church of the East.',[
('Institutional heritage','The Chaldean Diocese of St. Thomas the Apostle situates the Chaldean Catholic Church within the patrimony of the Church of the East. CNEWA supplies a further Catholic institutional overview of its history and Syriac liturgical context.'),
('Related histories','These accounts help readers understand relationships between churches without erasing distinct institutions. CNEWA also discusses the 1994 common Christological declaration and improved relations with the Assyrian Church of the East.'),
('Names and identity','"Chaldean" can appear in ecclesiastical and ethnic contexts. This article describes the church; it does not assume that every member uses the same ethnic self-description. Future coverage should include community voices alongside comparative historical scholarship.')],
['modern-chaldean','modern-chaldean-history'],['faith-and-churches','church-of-the-east','assyrian-identity'])

add('syriac-orthodox','The Syriac Orthodox Church','Faith & communities',
'A West Syriac Christian tradition with its own institutional and cultural heritage.',[
('An official self-description','The constitutional document provided by the Syriac Orthodox Patriarchate\'s Department of Syriac Studies uses the Syrian/Syriac Orthodox designation and describes the patriarch\'s institutional authority. It is a first-party source for the church\'s organisation.'),
('Language and writing','The wider West Syriac tradition includes distinctive script and pronunciation practices described in specialist Syriac language resources. These are linguistic and liturgical categories; they should not be turned into fixed ethnic boundaries.'),
('Scope of this entry','A full history of the church requires more than a constitutional document. Later stages should add comparative scholarship on theology, monasteries, architecture and regional communities. Apostolic foundation traditions should be attributed as traditions, and ethnic self-designations should be recorded with care.')],
['modern-syriac-constitution','gedsh-syriac'],['faith-and-churches','surayt','syriac-scripts'])

add('akitu','Akitu and Kha b-Nisan','Arts & traditions',
'New Year celebrations as a meeting place for heritage, music, food and community.',[
('A living celebration','Modern Assyrian New Year celebrations, often called Akitu or Kha b-Nisan, bring people together in homeland and diaspora settings. SBS Assyrian\'s reporting documents festivals that combine performance, books, food, art and community organisations.'),
('Ancient references, modern practice','Contemporary celebrations draw meaning from ancient Mesopotamian heritage. That connection should not be expanded into a claim that every ritual, date or calendar reckoning has continued unchanged since antiquity. Ancient festival practices and modern cultural renewal need their own evidence.'),
('Documenting an event','A useful festival record identifies the year, organiser, place, performers and community voices. The Sydney event linked below is historical reporting, not a current event listing. Future coverage can build a dated festival archive, with photographs and recordings used only with appropriate permissions.')],
['modern-akitu','modern-festival-sydney'],['dance','music','kileche'])

add('dance','Khigga and Assyrian dance','Arts & traditions',
'Shared steps, regional styles and the social life of dancing together.',[
('A practice carried by people','Dance is an active part of Assyrian cultural life at celebrations and community performances. The Assyrian Student Association of Arizona\'s Akitu Dance Crew describes workshops and names several dances, including Khigga Yaqoora, Gubare, Sheikhani and Bablaka.'),
('Variation matters','Dance names, spelling, tempo and steps can vary by region, teacher and performance context. A staged choreography is not necessarily identical to the way a family dances at a wedding. Each recorded example should identify the performers and setting.'),
('Learning and preservation','The best future dance entries will combine a practitioner\'s explanation with permission-cleared video, a named variation and musical context. Modern practice does not need an unprovable ancient date to be culturally meaningful. This introduction avoids claims that a particular sequence of steps has survived unchanged for millennia.')],
['modern-dance'],['akitu','music','oral-history'],image='khigga')

add('kileche','Kileche and the family table','Arts & traditions',
'Food offers a way into family memory, celebration and everyday cultural knowledge.',[
('A family account','Evan Yako\'s SBS Food feature presents kileche, Assyrian cookies, through his relationship with his mother Helen and the transmission of family food knowledge. It connects a recipe to a particular person\'s experience rather than treating food as an anonymous list of ingredients.'),
('Many versions can belong','Names, fillings, shapes and methods may differ between households and regions. A useful food archive records whose recipe it is, where they learnt it and when it is made. One family\'s version need not be declared the only authentic one.'),
('From link to cultural record','The original recipe remains on SBS. This archive does not reproduce its complete directions. Future contributions could pair a contributor-owned recipe with photographs, an interview and permission for publication, allowing cooking knowledge to be preserved alongside the story of the person who shares it.')],
['modern-kileche'],['oral-history','akitu','australia'])

add('music','Music, voice and cultural memory','Arts & traditions',
'Songs and performances connect language, public life and personal experience.',[
('Begin with listening','Assyrian music deserves coverage across artists, regions, genres and generations. SBS Assyrian offers interviews and audio programming that can help listeners encounter contemporary voices. Its Evin Agassi memorial reporting also documents how community members describe the significance of a musician.'),
('Build a careful discography','A reliable artist entry should identify recordings, credited writers and performers, release evidence and the source of biographical details. A fan upload can be a discovery lead, but its title and date are not automatically a verified discography.'),
('Preserve with permission','Lyrics, commercial recordings, cover art and videos usually have separate rights. The archive links to legitimate sources rather than copying complete songs or lyrics. Later stages can add authorised interviews, documented listening guides and artist-approved material.')],
['modern-radio','modern-agassi'],['evin-agassi','dance','endangered-languages'])

add('evin-agassi','Evin Agassi: a community remembers','People & diaspora',
'A starting point for documenting an influential musical voice through community testimony.',[
('A memorial record','SBS Assyrian reported on a memorial for Evin Agassi in Greenfield Park, Sydney, held on 22 September 2024. The report draws on attendance and community tributes. It offers evidence for how people remembered his music and its cultural meaning.'),
('Reception is part of history','Accounts of a musician should include listeners, migration settings and language as well as a list of recordings. Memorial testimony is valuable evidence of reception, while remaining different from an independently checked account of every event in an artist\'s life.'),
('A biography to develop','This is not a complete biography or discography. Future work should verify release details, credits and life dates against multiple reliable records, and seek permission for photographs and recordings. Where sources disagree, the archive should report the disagreement rather than silently select a convenient number.')],
['modern-agassi'],['music','australia','oral-history'])

add('oral-history','Oral histories: voices worth preserving','People & diaspora',
'Personal testimony brings migration, language and everyday life into the historical record.',[
('A voice in the archive','The NSW Migration Heritage Centre\'s interview with Sophia Kanna records her Assyrian background, her connection to Habbaniyah and her arrival in Sydney on 27 May 1964. It offers a specific account of home, work and language across migration.'),
('Memory and evidence','An oral history tells us what a person remembers and how they understand their experience. It can be checked against documents without losing the value of the speaker\'s perspective. Broad historical claims made during an interview still need independent evidence.'),
('A contribution method','For a future interview, record consent, the preferred display name, interview date, languages used and any access restrictions. Preserve the original recording and a transcript separately. Ask about places, objects, songs and ordinary routines as well as major events. Contributors should be able to discuss privacy before anything becomes public.')],
['modern-kanna'],['australia','assyrian-identity','endangered-languages'])

add('australia','Assyrian life in Australia','People & diaspora',
'Migration stories, language education and community institutions offer a grounded introduction.',[
('Start with a person','Sophia Kanna\'s archived migration history records her arrival in Sydney in 1964. Her account provides one route into the experience of building a life in Australia. It should not be treated as the single story of all Assyrian migration.'),
('Community institutions','The Assyrian Australian Association dates its establishment to 1969. Its account describes language education, scholarships, settlement work and cultural events. Its Resource Centre records establishment in 1984 in response to settlement needs around Fairfield and Liverpool.'),
('A wider picture to build','A fuller Australian section needs histories from multiple cities and generations, women\'s experiences, schools, arts, work, sport and public life. This edition does not publish an unsourced population total. Later demographic entries should specify census year, geography and the exact ancestry or language category being counted.')],
['modern-kanna','modern-aaa','modern-arc'],['assyrian-australian-association','oral-history','akitu'])

add('assyrian-australian-association','Assyrian Australian Association','People & diaspora',
'A community institution whose history illustrates the work of cultural and settlement support.',[
('An institutional history','The Assyrian Australian Association says it was founded in 1969. Its first-party history describes work spanning cultural events, language schools, scholarships and settlement support. These activities connect cultural transmission with the practical needs of a diaspora community.'),
('The Resource Centre','The association dates the Assyrian Resource Centre to 1984, established in response to needs in Fairfield and Liverpool. The linked source is the appropriate place to check its own account of services.'),
('Using this record','This entry is an introduction to the organisation, not an endorsement or a live service directory. Eligibility, programme availability and contact arrangements may change. Future institutional profiles should include dated records and preserve changes over time without turning old web copy into current advice.')],
['modern-aaa','modern-arc'],['australia','oral-history','assyrian-identity'])

add('simele','Simele, 1933','History & archaeology',
'An introduction to the massacre, its remembrance and the need for careful historical documentation.',[
('The historical subject','Assyrians were killed during the Iraqi military campaign of August 1933 in northern Iraq, including at Simele. The event occupies an important place in modern Assyrian historical memory. Assyrian Martyrs Day is observed on 7 August.'),
('Know the source type','The Assyrian Studies Association\'s 2020 letter calls for protection and impartial investigation of the site. The Assyrian Policy Institute\'s page presents a community advocacy perspective. These are useful sources for commemoration and preservation concerns, but an appeal is not a completed forensic investigation.'),
('What this edition does not settle','The archive does not publish a single casualty figure from these sources as a settled total. A full article needs comparative historical scholarship, contemporary records and explicit treatment of disputed estimates. Recognition and site-protection status also require dated verification; older campaign pages should not be presented as current policy.')],
['modern-simele-asa','modern-simele-api'],['sayfo','oral-history','heritage-stewardship'],note='Sensitive history. This foundation entry is limited in scope and requires a broader historical bibliography before expansion.')

add('sayfo','Sayfo and the First World War','History & archaeology',
'A research entry on mass violence against Assyrian, Syriac and Chaldean Christian communities.',[
('A field of historical research','The academic volume Let Them Not Return examines violence against Assyrian, Syriac and Chaldean Christians during and after the First World War, across borderland regions now in Turkey, Iran and Iraq. Sayfo is a central term in the study and remembrance of this history.'),
('Scholarship and testimony','The annotated source published as Sayfo - An Account of the Assyrian Genocide presents an eyewitness narrative associated with greater Tur Abdin. Eyewitness accounts preserve essential perspectives; they should be contextualised through location, date, authorship and comparison with other evidence.'),
('A careful expansion','This entry avoids an unsupported single casualty total and does not imply every region experienced identical events. A full treatment should distinguish local chronologies, survivor histories, displacement and later commemoration. The linked public catalogue descriptions are accessible, while access to complete books can vary. They are not marked here as freely licensed full texts.')],
['modern-sayfo','modern-sayfo-eyewitness'],['simele','oral-history','research-guide'],note='Sensitive history. Public book descriptions were consulted; this introduction does not claim a full review of the volumes.')

add('endangered-languages','Keeping living languages audible','Language & literature',
'Documentation, teaching and everyday use each play a role in language continuity.',[
('A documented need','Cambridge\'s NENA project describes the dialects it documents as highly endangered. It makes linguistic material available to researchers and speaker communities. The diversity within those records matters: saving a generic label is not the same as preserving a local variety.'),
('Record context as well as words','A useful recording includes the language variety, speaker\'s consent, date, place and the circumstances of the conversation. Stories, conversation and explanations of daily tasks can preserve forms that a vocabulary list misses. A transcription and translation make the recording easier to study.'),
('Teaching is another task','An archive can support learning but does not replace opportunities to speak. Match learners with materials for their intended variety, including the free Surayt course where appropriate. Future audio in this hub should come from consenting speakers and be reviewed for transcription and pronunciation.')],
['cambridge-nena','fu-surayt'],['sureth','surayt','oral-history'])

add('research-guide','How to research Assyrian history','Language & literature',
'A practical path from an interesting claim to evidence that another reader can check.',[
('Ask a precise question','Specify a person, place, language variety or period. "What does this tablet record?" is easier to investigate than a claim about an entire civilisation. Begin with a specialist overview, then follow its references to editions, catalogues and primary material.'),
('Choose the right collection','CDLI is useful for cuneiform records. Syriaca provides a reference portal for Syriac studies. syri.ac points to distributed manuscript resources, while eBethArke includes books, archives and recordings. These collections overlap without being interchangeable.'),
('Keep an evidence trail','Record author, title, publication date, stable URL and the page, folio or object identifier behind a claim. Distinguish a source\'s own words from your interpretation. If a translation or identification is disputed, name the alternatives and explain the basis.'),
('Access is not a licence','A page being readable does not make its text, images or recordings free to copy. Summarise in original language, link to the source and check reuse conditions for each asset. The source library records public discovery routes without promising unrestricted access to every underlying work.')],
['cdli','syriaca-intro','syriac-digimss','rutgers-ebetharke'],['manuscripts','heritage-stewardship','akkadian-and-cuneiform'])

add('heritage-stewardship','Caring for cultural heritage','Places & heritage',
'Documentation is a responsibility to objects, places and the people connected to them.',[
('Sites and collections','UNESCO\'s Ashur record is one starting point for understanding a major archaeological place. Museum records identify dispersed objects and their materials, dates and collection histories. Neither type of record replaces the voices of communities connected to the heritage.'),
('Digital survival','Manuscript photography and cataloguing can preserve access to evidence. HMML\'s collections illustrate the scale and complexity of that work. A digital surrogate still needs reliable metadata, storage and a clear relationship to the physical original.'),
('Practical principles','Preserve provenance, distinguish restoration from surviving fabric, record uncertainty and verify reproduction rights. Avoid publishing private family information or sensitive locations without consent. Future community contributions should document ownership, permissions and how the contributor wants to be credited.')],
['unesco-ashur','hmml-voobus','met-lion'],['ashur','manuscripts','oral-history'])

add('tur-abdin','Tur Abdin: language and a monastic landscape','Places & heritage',
'A limestone plateau in southeastern Turkey with a distinctive place in Syriac Christian history.',[
('A region with its own geography','Tur Abdin refers more precisely to the limestone plateau in the eastern part of Mardin province, between the Tigris and Nisibis. It is not simply another name for the whole province. Its villages, churches and monasteries form a connected historical landscape.'),
('Monasteries and institutions','The scholarly GEDSH account describes Hah as an early episcopal centre and the later importance of the monastery of Qartmin, known as Mor Gabriel. The region\'s ecclesiastical geography changed over time, including divisions and reunifications of jurisdictions. These institutional histories help explain why an individual building cannot stand for every period of the region\'s past.'),
('Language and memory','Surayt, also known as Turoyo, is associated with this region. Its spoken and written traditions extend into diaspora communities. The First World War violence remembered as Sayfo is also central to its modern history. Geography, language, monastic culture and displacement are connected subjects, each with its own evidence.')],
['gedsh-tur-abdin','gedsh-turoyo'],['surayt','sayfo','manuscripts'])

add('urmia','Urmia: language, printing and community','Places & heritage',
'A northwestern Iranian city whose schools, presses and speech belong in the modern cultural story.',[
('A city and its surrounding communities','Urmia lies west of Lake Urmia in northwestern Iran. The city and its region have an important place in the history of the Church of the East and modern Assyrian cultural life. City history and the histories of surrounding villages should be recorded separately.'),
('The nineteenth-century press','The GEDSH account describes the growth of missionary educational and publishing activity in the nineteenth century. The American mission press published Classical Syriac and Neo-Aramaic texts, including vernacular biblical translations and the periodical Zahrire d-Bahra, begun in 1849. Printing created new opportunities for circulation beyond the manuscript.'),
('A spoken record','Cambridge\'s Christian Urmi dialect page identifies fieldwork by Geoffrey Khan and links recordings such as village-life accounts, stories and material about the Assyrians of Urmi. These recordings preserve language use in context. They are a research and listening resource, with their own access and reuse conditions.'),
('Disruption and return','The First World War and the flight from Urmia in 1918 caused profound losses and displacement. Some Assyrian Christians later returned. This introduction avoids projecting an older source\'s community description into a present-day population estimate.')],
['gedsh-urmia','nena-urmi'],['syriac-printing','sureth','diaspora'])

add('nineveh-plain','The Nineveh Plain','Places & heritage',
'A region of living communities, language and religious heritage, distinct from the ancient city of Nineveh.',[
('Place and terminology','The Nineveh Plain is a modern regional setting for communities and settlements around the Mosul area. It should not be confused with the archaeological city of Nineveh, or treated as interchangeable with the full territory of the ancient Assyrian Empire.'),
('A linguistic landscape','The GEDSH entry on Sureth discusses North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic varieties associated especially with villages of the Mosul plain. A regional description still needs local detail: place names and language labels can have variant spellings and different histories of use.'),
('A dated community record','A UNAMI release from 3 October 2018 describes meetings in Bartella and Hamdaniya district with Christian and Shabak representatives. Reported concerns included public services, employment, political representation and the effects of conflict and demographic change. This is a dated record of those discussions, not a current assessment of every settlement or a travel advisory.'),
('The next level of detail','Future town entries should identify historic and current names, language varieties, institutions and sources for changes over time. Population and security information need explicit dates. Community histories should be represented through more than archaeological monuments alone.')],
['gedsh-sureth','unami-nineveh'],['nineveh','sureth','heritage-stewardship'])

add('diaspora','Across borders: the diaspora','People & diaspora',
'Migration creates new cultural centres while preserving, adapting and sometimes interrupting connections to earlier homes.',[
('Several migration histories','The Syriac Christian diaspora developed through different movements, rather than one departure at one date. The GEDSH overview identifies violence, political upheaval and limited educational or employment opportunities among the causes of migration. Displacement also occurred within the Middle East, not only towards Europe, the Americas or Australia.'),
('New centres of life','The same overview describes late nineteenth-century movement from the Urmia region towards the United States and later communities in places including Sweden, Germany and Australia. These are examples, not a complete census. Its older church and population listings should not be read as current totals.'),
('Culture in a new setting','Schools, churches, associations, media and family networks can create spaces for language and shared memory. The Assyrian Australian Association and Sophia Kanna\'s migration interview provide two Australian examples: one institutional, one personal.'),
('A global section to build','A fuller diaspora record needs local histories from multiple countries, accounts of return and continuing connections to homeland communities. It should include ordinary work, intergenerational experience and cultural creativity as well as displacement. The archive will need country-specific evidence before making demographic comparisons.')],
['gedsh-diaspora','modern-aaa','modern-kanna'],['australia','urmia','oral-history'])

add('syriac-printing','From manuscript to printing press','Language & literature',
'Type, publishing and later digital tools changed how Syriac and Neo-Aramaic texts could circulate.',[
('Early printed texts','The GEDSH history of printing distinguishes early Syriac woodcuts in European books from printing with type. It identifies Syriac type in an introductory language work printed at Pavia in 1539 and the Syriac New Testament printed at Vienna in 1555.'),
('Presses in several regions','A Syriac Psalter was printed at the Maronite monastery of Quzhayya in Lebanon in 1610. In nineteenth-century Urmia, mission printing supported vernacular texts as well as Classical Syriac. The newspaper Zahrire d-Bahra ran from 1849 to 1918. Other important presses operated in Mosul, Beirut and India.'),
('Print and a public readership','Printed religious and secular works connected publishers, readers and communities across distances. A publication\'s script, place of printing, language variety and intended audience each add context. Since the late twentieth century, digital composition has widened the practical possibilities for publishing in Syriac scripts.')],
['gedsh-printing'],['urmia','manuscripts','syriac-scripts'])

add('edessa','Edessa, Urhay and Urfa','Places & heritage',
'A city of trade, political change and literary importance in the development of Syriac.',[
('Several names, one changing city','Edessa, Syriac Urhay and modern Urfa refer to a city in southeastern Turkey. Its earlier settlement was known through forms such as Adme. Seleucus I rebuilt the settlement as a Hellenistic city in 304 BCE and named it Edessa.'),
('Aramaic and Syriac Christianity','The city\'s Aramaic variety is attested in inscriptions, documents and coins, and became the literary language of Syriac Christianity. Its Christian history includes diverse and sometimes competing traditions. A city associated with a major literary language was never simply a single, uniform intellectual community.'),
('A place in literary history','Ephrem\'s later career connects Edessa with one of the best-known figures in Syriac poetry and theology. The city\'s history can be studied through chronicles, inscriptions, texts and archaeology. Foundation legends and later associations should be distinguished from independently dated evidence.')],
['gedsh-edessa'],['classical-syriac','ephrem','tur-abdin'])


# Milestone 2: original, sourced historical and social-depth guides.
# Sections are explicitly mapped to their references in data/section-evidence.json.
add("ashurnasirpal-ii", "Ashurnasirpal II: rebuilding Kalhu", "History & archaeology",
    "A ninth-century ruler whose campaigns, palace building and inscriptions reshaped the Assyrian royal landscape.", [
    ("The reign and its evidence", "Ashurnasirpal II ruled approximately 883-859 BCE. The Metropolitan Museum of Art places his reign within a renewed period of Neo-Assyrian territorial expansion. The dates are modern historical coordinates based on ancient evidence, not dates supplied by the Bible. Much surviving material was commissioned to present the ruler as victorious and legitimate. It gives historians indispensable information about places and royal priorities, but its literary self-presentation should be read critically."),
    ("Why Kalhu mattered", "Ashurnasirpal made Kalhu, now usually called Nimrud, a major royal capital. Moving the political centre did not erase the older city of Ashur, which continued to matter as a religious centre. Palace rooms, sculptured orthostats and inscriptions reveal a deliberate effort to make authority visible to visitors and officials. The modern distribution of these objects among museums also belongs to their history; a displayed slab must be traced to its original room where possible."),
    ("Palace art as a political record", "Protective figures, hunting scenes, military imagery and formal royal figures helped structure encounters with the king's power. They are not neutral photographs of daily life. The Metropolitan Museum's Assyrian Sculpture Court reuses objects in a modern arrangement and warns against treating that display as a complete reconstruction of the original palace. A precise study should pair each panel with its object number, findspot, text and original architectural position."),
    ("Read beyond the king", "Royal inscriptions are disproportionately visible, while ordinary residents, craftsmen, women and displaced populations appear less often in the material most readily accessible to visitors. The reign therefore needs archaeological and administrative evidence alongside monumental royal sources. The introductory account does not invent a complete picture of social life from palace art, nor does it equate Ashurnasirpal's state with every later population called Assyrian.")
], ["met-assyria","met-rulers-assyria","met-court","met-relief"], ["nimrud","royal-reliefs","ancient-assyria","shalmaneser-iii"], note="A historical synthesis of royal and archaeological material; no named biblical passage is asserted for this ruler.")

add("shalmaneser-iii", "Shalmaneser III and the western campaigns", "History & archaeology",
    "Campaigning, inscriptions and the changing relationships between Assyria and western kingdoms in the ninth century BCE.", [
    ("A long reign", "Shalmaneser III succeeded Ashurnasirpal II and ruled approximately 858-824 BCE. The Metropolitan Museum places him among the kings who consolidated and extended Assyrian ambitions in Syria, the Levant and Anatolia. A reign of this length should not be collapsed into one victorious campaign; relations with neighbouring states shifted across decades, and the kings' own inscriptions prioritise achievements and claims useful to court audiences."),
    ("An inscriptional itinerary", "The Royal Inscriptions of Assyria Online catalogue provides a named collection of Shalmaneser III's royal inscriptions. An inscription can locate a reported campaign or tribute episode in a specific official narrative, but the text must be tied to an edition and passage before being quoted as direct evidence for a disputed event. Campaign lists do not automatically establish continuous annexation or effective rule over every named country."),
    ("Kingdoms and coalitions", "The Metropolitan Museum's regional chronology describes opposition to Shalmaneser's army in 853 BCE involving a coalition that included Ahab of Israel. This is an external historical reconstruction drawing on Assyrian evidence. The biblical books should not be made to say that they directly describe this particular confrontation unless the cited passage actually does so. Military interaction and later tribute should also be distinguished from provincial administration."),
    ("What comes after a campaign", "Military inscriptions illuminate royal priorities more readily than individual villagers' experiences. Trade, tribute, alliances, and the need to maintain routes and garrisons made durable control more complicated than conquest rhetoric. To deepen this guide, the next research pass should identify specific inscriptions and the peoples they name, then compare those accounts with regional archaeology and dated records instead of relying only on modern narrative summaries.")
], ["met-assyria","met-rulers-assyria","met-levant-chronology","oracc-shalmaneser"], ["ashurnasirpal-ii","tiglath-pileser-iii","assyrian-governance","royal-reliefs"])

add("tiglath-pileser-iii", "Tiglath-pileser III: empire and provinces", "History & archaeology",
    "A ruler whose campaigns and administrative changes transformed Assyrian relations with neighbouring states.", [
    ("A turning point in imperial rule", "Tiglath-pileser III ruled from the middle of the eighth century BCE, approximately 745-727 BCE. The Assyrian Empire Builders research project presents his conquests and administrative changes as a decisive phase in the expansion of direct Assyrian rule. Such descriptions are scholarly assessments of a political process; the empire did not acquire one uniform legal or cultural system overnight. Royal inscriptions, letters and provincial evidence document different aspects of the transformation."),
    ("Direct rule and dependent kingdoms", "The empire governed some conquered areas through appointed officials and provinces, while other rulers retained local thrones under Assyrian pressure. Confusing these arrangements overstates what maps and tribute claims can demonstrate. The Metropolitan Museum's eastern Mediterranean chronology describes directly administered provinces and policies of mass displacement. These mechanisms should be described together: political power operated through institutions and people, not only border expansion."),
    ("The Bible names the king", "Second Kings 15:29 reports Tiglath-pileser's seizure of places in northern Israel and deportations. Second Kings 16:7-9 records Ahaz's appeal to the Assyrian king and the taking of Damascus. These verses are the primary biblical evidence for what Kings says; a historical chronology or geographic extent supplied by Assyrian records is a separate comparison. The account in 2 Chronicles 28:16-21 provides a different emphasis on the consequences for Ahaz."),
    ("Names across records", "Biblical books preserve forms such as Pul and Tilgath-pilneser. Identifying ancient persons across differently transmitted names requires philological and historical comparison; a spelling resemblance by itself is insufficient. The archive retains each biblical form in its own context and uses Tiglath-pileser III as the conventional historical name where appropriate. The companion biblical guide and inscription collections should be read side by side, with every interpretive identification made explicit.")
], ["bible-webbe","aebp-tiglath","met-rulers-assyria","met-levant-chronology","aebp-governance"], ["bible-pul-menahem","bible-tiglath-pileser","shalmaneser-v","assyrian-governance","assyrian-deportation"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "tiglath-pileser-iii")["passages"] = [["2KI",15,29,29],["2KI",16,7,9],["2CH",28,16,21]]

add("shalmaneser-v", "Shalmaneser V and the Samaria question", "History & archaeology",
    "A short reign, a contested succession and the care required when discussing Samaria's fall.", [
    ("The successor to Tiglath-pileser", "Shalmaneser V followed Tiglath-pileser III and ruled approximately 726-722 BCE. Specialist analysis in Assyrian Empire Builders discusses his earlier position as crown prince, his royal correspondence and his short reign. Letters offer unusually practical evidence of administration that royal victory texts rarely show. The available records should not be mistaken for a complete biography, and hypotheses about his death or the succession must be labelled as such."),
    ("The historical record is uneven", "Only a small amount of official inscriptional material survives from Shalmaneser V's reign. The Assyrian Empire Builders study discusses the transition to Sargon II, whose ascent involved conflict. Later claims made by Sargon about his predecessor need particular care because they belong to a politically interested successor's narrative. Differences between sparse contemporary sources and later royal presentations are part of the historical problem, not editorial clutter to remove."),
    ("Samaria in Kings", "Second Kings 17:3-6 names Shalmaneser in the account of Hoshea's tribute and describes the siege, capture and deportation associated with Samaria. The parallel account in 2 Kings 18:9-12 should be read in full. Identifying the named king with Shalmaneser V draws on historical chronology; deciding which ruler took the city and which later ruler claimed the victory also requires independent Assyrian sources. The archive must not make these distinct questions silently identical."),
    ("A necessary uncertainty", "Assyrian Empire Builders treats evidence for changes in western provincial administration under Shalmaneser and the violent accession of Sargon. These scholarly reconstructions do not remove every chronological difficulty. A responsible guide states what Kings reports, what the surviving Assyrian materials support, and where the chronology remains disputed. The next source pass should identify the precise royal lines and dated records at issue.")
], ["bible-webbe","aebp-shalmaneser-v","aebp-sargon","met-rulers-assyria"], ["bible-samaria","bible-deportation","sargon-ii","tiglath-pileser-iii","fall-of-nineveh"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "shalmaneser-v")["passages"] = [["2KI",17,3,6],["2KI",18,9,12]]

add("sargon-ii", "Sargon II: king, campaigns and a new capital", "History & archaeology",
    "A ruler known from royal inscriptions, Khorsabad archaeology and the single explicit Sargon reference in Isaiah.", [
    ("A contested beginning", "Sargon II came to power in the late eighth century BCE and ruled approximately 721-705 BCE. Assyrian Empire Builders places his accession amid conflict following Shalmaneser V. Royal accounts legitimising a new reign are not independent records of the predecessor's conduct. They require comparison with letters, provincial evidence and later historical interpretation. This distinction is especially important in discussing Samaria, which was also part of the political transition."),
    ("Dur-Sharrukin as a royal statement", "Sargon founded Dur-Sharrukin, the archaeological site called Khorsabad, as a new royal centre. The French Ministry of Culture's site account documents the city, palace and modern excavations. Architecture, inscriptions and monumental sculpture show the project's political ambition. What survives today is incomplete; a reconstruction must distinguish original remains, nineteenth-century records and modern display. Sargon's planned capital should not be treated as a map of the whole empire."),
    ("Sargon in Isaiah", "Isaiah 20:1 explicitly names Sargon king of Assyria when it describes the Tartan's action against Ashdod. The remaining verses present Isaiah's enacted sign and a warning concerning reliance on Egypt and Cush. Isaiah is the primary source for the biblical narrative. The connection between Sargon and the archaeological city Dur-Sharrukin comes from a different body of evidence and is not stated in Isaiah."),
    ("Claims, chronology and source roles", "Historical essays and the royal inscription corpus can explain Sargon's campaigns and the administrative context of his reign. They do not establish the literal truth of every claim in royal rhetoric. A detailed comparison should specify which inscription, tablet or letter describes a given event, identify its date and authorship where known, and separate reports of taking a city from proof of long-term control.")
], ["bible-webbe","aebp-sargon","aebp-shalmaneser-v","khorsabad-ministry","oracc-rinap"], ["khorsabad","bible-sargon","shalmaneser-v","sennacherib","assyrian-governance"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "sargon-ii")["passages"] = [["ISA",20,1,6]]

add("sennacherib", "Sennacherib: Nineveh and Judah", "History & archaeology",
    "Palace building, the campaign against Judah and the distinct testimony of biblical and Assyrian records.", [
    ("The capital at Nineveh", "Sennacherib succeeded Sargon II and reigned approximately 704-681 BCE. The Metropolitan Museum of Art identifies his development of Nineveh as a major transformation of the Assyrian capital. The city included the palace complex described in royal texts and represented in excavated architectural remains. The phrase 'palace without rival' comes from royal presentation and should be recognised as an expression of the king's ambitions, not an independent measure of architectural supremacy."),
    ("Lachish in stone", "The British Museum catalogues gypsum palace panels from Nineveh depicting an Assyrian assault on the Judean city of Lachish. One registered panel, number 124904, depicts slingers and archers; another, 124908, shows prisoners and booty. Their findspot in the palace and their museum numbers make them independently locatable artefacts. They establish a material record of how the Assyrian court commemorated the campaign, not a complete account of every civilian's experience."),
    ("The account in Kings and Isaiah", "Second Kings 18:13-16 reports Sennacherib's seizure of fortified cities and the tribute paid by Hezekiah. The narrative continues through the Assyrian delegation, Hezekiah's prayer and the divine deliverance reported in 2 Kings 19; Isaiah 36-37 preserves a parallel account. These passages are primary for the biblical account, including its theological claims. Details and numbers in the Bible should not be rewritten from an Assyrian inscription, or vice versa, without explaining the different witnesses."),
    ("How to compare the accounts", "The royal inscriptions corpus is a route to the Assyrian king's own presentation of campaigns; biblical writings speak within their own historical and theological framework. Agreement on particular people or places does not demonstrate agreement on every reported outcome. A useful future comparison will cite exact inscription identifiers and lines, place them beside specific verses and explicitly discuss differences in motive, date, tribute and narrative focus.")
], ["bible-webbe","met-assyria","met-nineveh-history","bm-lachish-panel","bm-lachish-deportees","oracc-rinap"], ["nineveh","bible-sennacherib-invasion","bible-hezekiah-deliverance","bible-rabshakeh","esarhaddon"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "sennacherib")["passages"] = [["2KI",18,13,37],["2KI",19,1,37],["ISA",36,1,22],["ISA",37,1,38]]

add("esarhaddon", "Esarhaddon: succession and Egypt", "History & archaeology",
    "From the murder of Sennacherib to the campaigns in Egypt and the question of royal succession.", [
    ("The succession in Scripture", "Second Kings 19:36-37 describes Sennacherib's return to Nineveh, his killing by two sons and the succession of Esarhaddon. Isaiah 37:37-38 provides the parallel biblical account. This is what the passages explicitly report. The names and sequence can be investigated through other sources, but the Bible's wording should be presented before the historical comparison, and a later scholarly reconstruction should not be inserted into the quotation."),
    ("The ruler and the wider empire", "Esarhaddon ruled approximately 680-669 BCE. The Metropolitan Museum of Art describes his Egyptian campaign and the capture of Memphis in 671 BCE, followed by his death during a later expedition. These are externally reconstructed historical events, not subjects of the cited succession verses. Campaign records and royal inscriptions are valuable but must be read with attention to royal self-representation and to the limits of military control."),
    ("Ezra's settlement memory", "Ezra 4:2 preserves the speech of people who refer to settlement in the land during Esar Haddon's time. Their claim belongs to a later biblical narrative and should be read with the surrounding verses. It is not a complete census of people moved by Assyria, nor does the passage provide the administrative files or the exact scale of that resettlement. Such details require independent historical sources."),
    ("Beyond one individual", "Royal succession drew together dynastic politics, provinces, military forces and the court. An overview that speaks only of conquest omits the administrative apparatus that made campaigns possible. The RINAP corpus provides a route into texts attributed to Esarhaddon, while comparative institutional studies explain the types of evidence used. Future detailed claims require exact inscription lines, rather than a blanket citation to the entire collection.")
], ["bible-webbe","met-assyria","met-rulers-assyria","oracc-rinap","aebp-deportation","aebp-governance"], ["sennacherib","bible-esarhaddon","ashurbanipal","assyrian-governance","assyrian-deportation"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "esarhaddon")["passages"] = [["2KI",19,36,37],["ISA",37,37,38],["EZR",4,1,5]]

add("ashurbanipal", "Ashurbanipal: king and the tablet collections", "History & archaeology",
    "Political power and scholarly collecting at Nineveh during the later Neo-Assyrian empire.", [
    ("A late imperial ruler", "Ashurbanipal succeeded Esarhaddon in the seventh century BCE. The Metropolitan Museum of Art describes a reign involving military action in Egypt, Babylonia and Elam. Dates differ slightly across modern chronologies and catalogues, which is a reason to identify the dating system rather than present one endpoint as certain. Courtly evidence is extensive but strongly shaped by the interests of the ruler and palace."),
    ("A library is not one room", "The scholarly texts found at Nineveh include copied literary, ritual and learned works. Calling the collection the 'Library of Ashurbanipal' is conventional, but it must not conjure a modern lending institution. The Metropolitan Museum and specialist library studies place these materials within a much broader ancient tradition of scribal learning, copying and the movement of tablets. The collection is evidence of institutions and texts, not simply one king's personal reading preferences."),
    ("A tablet that can be checked", "The British Museum catalogues object 1881,0204.188, a clay tablet containing a prayer and identified by its colophon as copied for Ashurbanipal's palace library. The museum records its provenance and physical characteristics. This specific object is a stronger traceable starting point than an uncited claim that an entire library survives intact. The inscribed wording and scribal context still require a qualified edition for detailed interpretation."),
    ("After the reign", "The end of Assyrian imperial power followed periods of internal and external conflict. It should not be attributed to one simple cause or represented as proof of an abrupt disappearance of all people, language or culture associated with the region. Royal inscriptions, chronicles and archaeological strata offer different perspectives on these changes. The history of the collapse belongs in its own sourced article instead of being a single dramatic closing sentence here.")
], ["met-assyria","met-rulers-assyria","met-nineveh-history","yale-libraries","bm-ashurbanipal-tablet","bm-chronicle"], ["nineveh","esarhaddon","fall-of-nineveh","manuscripts","akkadian-and-cuneiform"])

add("assyrian-governance", "Running the Neo-Assyrian empire", "History & archaeology",
    "Governors, provinces, royal correspondence and the institutions behind military power.", [
    ("A government beyond the palace image", "Royal inscriptions frequently place the king at the centre of every achievement. Administrative letters and institutional texts show a more complex reality. The Assyrian Empire Builders project describes powerful officials, appointed governors, military officers and scribes who managed affairs across a large territory. Political decisions travelled through people and documents, not by royal presence everywhere. This evidence helps correct an account built solely from carved victory scenes."),
    ("Different relationships with regions", "Some places were organised as directly administered provinces; other kingdoms retained local rulers while accepting Assyrian demands and political pressure. A map depicting both in one colour can conceal important legal and practical differences. Governors were appointed by the king, yet exercised substantial authority in their jurisdictions. The historian must distinguish a claimed campaign, a tribute relationship, a vassal kingdom and a province when assessing the territorial reach of the state."),
    ("Scribes, messages and delegated work", "Administrative records and letters illuminate the work of provincial officials and palace scribes. The Assyrian Empire Builders study points to organised systems that transmitted instructions and allowed deputies to act in a superior's absence. Such mechanisms make the empire more intelligible than a succession of wars alone. They also reveal why isolated palace proclamations do not give a complete picture of political practice."),
    ("What a source can establish", "An official text may attest that a king ordered an action, while a letter can record a response, obstacle or disagreement. Neither guarantees that every intended result was achieved. For each administrative claim, the archive should identify who wrote the document, its date where established, the office or place involved and the edition or catalogue reference. Surviving letters come from uneven locations and should not be used as a comprehensive census of imperial activity.")
], ["aebp-governance","aebp-countries","oracc-rinap"], ["tiglath-pileser-iii","sargon-ii","assyrian-deportation","imperial-languages","ancient-assyria"])

add("assyrian-deportation", "Deportation and resettlement in Assyria", "History & archaeology",
    "State-directed migration, displacement, labour and the difficult evidence of imperial population policies.", [
    ("People moved by imperial policy", "Assyrian rulers organised the movement of populations across conquered and long-held territories. Specialist study in Assyrian Empire Builders examines relocation as part of imperial administration, agriculture and political control. These policies were not simply voluntary migrations. They disrupted existing communities and placed people under state power, even where official accounts described the outcome favourably. Different movements had different motives, and no single narrative describes the experiences of every relocated person."),
    ("The perspective of the state", "Royal inscriptions and images often present displacement as a successful administrative achievement, with particular interest in labour, skilled crafts and settled production. These are sources for official policy and representation, not a direct statement from each affected household. Assyrian Empire Builders discusses the arrangements made for some deportees, but that evidence must not be used to dismiss coercion, danger, family loss or other experiences that are poorly recorded."),
    ("Deportation in the Bible", "Second Kings 17:6 records Israelites being taken from Samaria to places in the Assyrian world; 2 Kings 17:24 describes incoming settlers. First Chronicles 5:25-26 also connects eastern Israelite groups with Assyrian deportation. These are biblical primary references for the movements they describe. Later scholarship addresses how such policies operated on a wider scale. Biblical texts and royal administrative perspectives should remain separately attributable."),
    ("Numbers and human scale", "Large figures in imperial reports are not automatically verified totals. The numbers may be stylised or incomplete, while undocumented people remain invisible. The archive should prefer precisely identified records and explain how estimates are calculated, instead of repeating impressive figures without context. Individual letters, objects and biblical passages can reveal particular aspects of displacement, but they cannot reconstruct every fate or justify projecting modern identity labels uncritically backwards.")
], ["bible-webbe","aebp-deportation","bm-lachish-deportees"], ["bible-deportation","bible-samaria","assyrian-governance","tiglath-pileser-iii","shalmaneser-v"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "assyrian-deportation")["passages"] = [["2KI",17,6,6],["2KI",17,24,24],["1CH",5,25,26]]

add("assyrian-religious-life", "Religion, temples and kingship in ancient Assyria", "Faith & communities",
    "The city and deity Ashur, temple institutions and the limits of reconstructing ancient belief from elite texts.", [
    ("Ashur: city, land and deity", "Ancient Ashur was a city and a central religious location, as well as the name associated with the chief Assyrian god. UNESCO documents temples, urban remains, kingship and the wider archaeological setting. The recurrence of a name across these domains calls for careful context; it does not turn every use of Ashur into a statement about the same subject. This account concerns ancient religious institutions, not the modern Syriac Christian faith traditions discussed elsewhere in the archive."),
    ("Temples and political authority", "UNESCO records the religious and political importance of Ashur as well as the remains of major temple buildings and places linked with royal ceremonies. Palaces and temple estates could have distinct functions, and the movement of a royal residence to Kalhu or Nineveh did not erase Ashur's symbolic role. Surviving architectural phases should be dated separately instead of treating a site as a single unchanging sanctuary over millennia."),
    ("Ritual texts and scribal learning", "A British Museum tablet from Ashurbanipal's collection preserves a copied prayer. This specific object documents scholarly transmission of a religious text in a palace setting; it is not a complete picture of household practice. Written texts, excavated spaces and images each reveal different aspects of ancient religion. Any assertion about what 'all Assyrians believed' should be checked for period, region, social group and evidence."),
    ("Separate historical and theological questions", "The Bible contains its own accounts of Assyrian kings, gods and conflict. For biblical narratives, those cited passages are the primary textual references. Reconstructing historical Assyrian cult practice is a distinct question requiring archaeological and Assyriological evidence. Neither source category should be made to speak beyond its content. Later Christian Assyrian identities and practices require their own much later documented history, not an unsupported claim of identical religious rites.")
], ["unesco-ashur","met-assyria","bm-ashurbanipal-tablet","yale-libraries"], ["ashur","faith-and-churches","assyrian-identity","ashurbanipal","ancient-assyria"], note="Ancient Mesopotamian religious institutions are historically distinct from later Christian traditions.")

add("imperial-languages", "Languages and writing across the Assyrian empire", "Language & literature",
    "Assyrian Akkadian, Aramaic, royal inscriptions and the multilingual world of administration.", [
    ("More than one language", "Assyrian Akkadian was written in cuneiform, but the regions under Assyrian influence contained many languages. The empire's records preserve several forms of writing and varied local traditions. An Akkadian inscription makes a specific claim about the language of that text, not the everyday language of every resident. Later Aramaic and Classical Syriac developed in different historical settings and cannot be treated as identical to ancient Assyrian Akkadian."),
    ("Aramaic and an expanding empire", "The Metropolitan Museum's regional chronology connects Assyrian imperial expansion and population movements with the wider spread of Aramaic language and alphabetic writing. This is a historical synthesis, not an assertion that everyone adopted a single spoken dialect simultaneously. Administrative correspondence and objects need to be examined individually for language, script, geography and date."),
    ("The biblical language exchange", "Second Kings 18:26 reports the officials' request that the Assyrian representative speak in Aramaic because they understood it, rather than in the language of Judah before the listeners on the wall. Isaiah 36:11 gives a parallel account. These passages are the direct biblical evidence for the narrated exchange. They do not establish that the spoken Aramaic of the episode was the same as a contemporary Neo-Aramaic variety."),
    ("Scripts and continuity need precision", "Cuneiform is a writing system, not a synonym for one language; Syriac script belongs to another historical writing tradition. The Syriac literary language is a form of Aramaic and requires separate sources when treated in detail. A responsible language history follows demonstrable texts, linguistic change and named varieties rather than using the survival of a script to prove that all spoken forms stayed unchanged. The longer language articles provide those necessary distinctions.")
], ["bible-webbe","cambridge-mesopotamian-languages","gedsh-aramaic","gedsh-syriac","met-levant-chronology"], ["akkadian-and-cuneiform","aramaic","classical-syriac","sureth","bible-rabshakeh"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "imperial-languages")["passages"] = [["2KI",18,26,26],["ISA",36,11,11]]

add("fall-of-nineveh", "The fall of Nineveh and the end of Assyrian imperial rule", "History & archaeology",
    "The late seventh-century collapse, its historical sources and the difference between political defeat and cultural disappearance.", [
    ("The final imperial decades", "The Neo-Assyrian state suffered severe political and military reversals in the later seventh century BCE. The Metropolitan Museum describes attacks involving Babylonian and Median forces. There was no single instant at which the complex system of cities, provinces and royal households ceased to matter. Dates for separate campaigns, sieges and rulers must be reconstructed from chronicles, inscriptions and archaeology, not from the simple label 'fall of Assyria'."),
    ("Nineveh in 612 BCE", "Nineveh was captured in 612 BCE during the wars that ended Assyrian imperial domination. Historical narratives draw on Babylonian chronicle traditions as well as destruction and later activity at the site. The referenced Babylonian Chronicle tablet is one surviving material route into this chronology, but summaries and photographed objects are not substitutes for a line-by-line edition of the tablet. The archive should preserve the distinction between a dated chronicle report and an interpretation of archaeological destruction."),
    ("The Bible's prophetic witness", "Nahum presents an oracle about Nineveh, and Zephaniah 2:13-15 announces the city's desolation. These are the primary biblical references for what those books say. Neither passage supplies an explicit date of 612 BCE, identifies every besieging force or narrates the Babylonian Chronicle. A comparison may show how later historians relate prophecy and historical events, but those added dates must be credited to historical evidence rather than attributed to the verse itself."),
    ("Why 609 BCE also matters", "The Metropolitan Museum's regional chronology identifies a further Assyrian stand at Harran in 609 BCE. This distinguishes the fall of the major capital in 612 BCE from the end of attempts to retain or restore imperial rule. Confusing the two dates shortens the narrative artificially. The collapse of a political state likewise does not prove that every local person, language or cultural practice vanished with it.")
], ["bible-webbe","met-assyria","met-rulers-assyria","bm-chronicle","met-nineveh-history","met-levant-chronology"], ["nineveh","bible-nahum","bible-zephaniah","post-imperial-assyria","ashurbanipal"])

next(a for a in ARTICLES if a["slug"] == "fall-of-nineveh")["passages"] = [["NAH",1,1,1],["ZEP",2,13,15]]

add("post-imperial-assyria", "After the empire: Assyria's changing regions", "History & archaeology",
    "Political change after the fall of the Assyrian state and the evidence needed to discuss later communities responsibly.", [
    ("An empire ends, a region remains", "The destruction of royal centres and end of Assyrian imperial government in the late seventh century BCE did not cause the landscape or all its inhabitants to disappear. Yet one cannot infer the precise ancestry or unbroken institutions of later communities merely from survival of a place name. To study subsequent centuries, historians need dated settlement layers, inscriptions, documentary sources and the histories of successive states and religious traditions."),
    ("Ashur's later archaeological life", "UNESCO describes the ancient city of Ashur as having later occupation and revival under Parthian rule. Its architectural and burial evidence provides a concrete example of post-imperial activity at a place central to earlier Assyrian history. Later structures cannot simply be labelled 'Neo-Assyrian' because they stand at Ashur. Each archaeological phase must be analysed for date, use and context; continuity of occupation and continuity of particular institutions are different claims."),
    ("Changing imperial contexts", "After the period of Assyrian rule, northern Mesopotamia was affected by successive powers and shifting cultural networks. The regional chronology supplied by the Metropolitan Museum situates late Assyrian defeat within the rise of Babylonia and later imperial changes. Extending the article into Persian, Hellenistic and Parthian centuries will require further location-specific references rather than assuming that general eastern Mediterranean history explains every settlement in Assyria."),
    ("Language, faith and modern identity", "Later Aramaic and Syriac textual traditions matter deeply to the history of communities associated with this region, but they belong to distinct historical periods and sources. A rigorous account can explore relationships among ancient geography, Syriac Christian literatures and modern Assyrian identities while acknowledging disputed interpretations. The continued existence of communities does not itself verify any single unchanged ethnic label, language or religious practice across twenty-six centuries.")
], ["met-assyria","unesco-ashur","met-levant-chronology","gedsh-aramaic","gedsh-assyrians"], ["ashur","ancient-assyria","fall-of-nineveh","edessa","assyrian-identity"], note="Political continuity, population histories and cultural identity require separate lines of evidence; no unbroken lineage is asserted.")



# Milestone 4: carefully attributed language and script research, not unreviewed pronunciation lessons.
add("cuneiform-reading-methods", "Reading Assyrian cuneiform responsibly", "Language & literature",
    "How an ancient tablet becomes a catalogue record, sign transcription, linguistic interpretation and historical argument.", [
    ("From object to identified text", "A clay tablet or stone inscription is a physical object first. The University of Cambridge explains that cuneiform was employed for multiple ancient Mesopotamian languages, including Assyrian and Babylonian varieties of Akkadian. A wedge-shaped sign does not identify a spoken language by itself. The object's findspot, accession number, physical condition, palaeography and date must be considered before treating a reconstructed text as an ancient Assyrian statement."),
    ("Signs, transliteration and translation", "A sign-by-sign transcription or transliteration attempts to record what is written; a normalised Akkadian reading adds philological interpretation; an English translation is another interpretive stage. They should not be presented as identical layers. A broken tablet may contain restored passages marked by editorial conventions, and a royal inscription may use formulaic language. Linking to CDLI identifies a place to search records and editions, not a licence to generate unattested original-language lines."),
    ("Assyrian and Babylonian as Akkadian traditions", "Cambridge identifies Assyrian and Babylonian as closely related linguistic varieties often grouped as Akkadian. This ancient branch is not the same language as modern Assyrian Neo-Aramaic. Both belong to Semitic history in a broad sense, but genealogical classification and the lives of present-day communities answer different questions. This distinction protects linguistic scholarship from a mistaken equation of two texts separated by many centuries."),
    ("Check the claim against the edition", "For a historical argument, record the artefact or inscription identifier, repository, ancient text edition, passage or line numbers, translation credit and uncertainty. Ask whether a statement is the king's claim, an administrative report or a later scholar's synthesis. Exact readings can be disputed; the archive should explain alternatives instead of silently replacing the tablet with a modern paraphrase. Full research comparison remains a future specialist task.")
], ["cambridge-mesopotamian-languages","cdli","oracc-rinap","gedsh-aramaic"], ["akkadian-and-cuneiform","old-assyrian-trade","imperial-languages","research-guide"])

add("syriac-script-histories", "Estrangela, Serto and East Syriac hands", "Language & literature",
    "Three connected manuscript traditions with changing letterforms, regional usage and imperfect boundaries.", [
    ("One Unicode script, several written styles", "The Unicode Standard encodes Syriac characters as one writing system. Estrangela, Serto and East Syriac are type or handwriting traditions, not three separately encoded alphabets. A Unicode letter such as ܐ (ALAPH, U+0710) remains the same code point even when a typeface renders a different historical letterform. Font style cannot, by itself, tell readers what language variety or personal identity a scribe had."),
    ("Estrangela over time", "HMML's manuscript-based lessons illustrate early and later Estrangela hands. Letter proportions, joins and stroke details changed over time, and manuscripts can show features found in more than one named style. An illustrated chart of modern display letters therefore cannot authenticate an old manuscript. A responsible comparison identifies a specific dated or approximately dated folio and the diagnostic features on that page."),
    ("Serto and East Syriac", "Serto became characteristic of many West Syriac manuscripts; East Syriac developed its own common shapes and vocalisation conventions. HMML cautions that boundaries are not rigid: a historical hand may retain Estrangela forms alongside Serto or East Syriac ones. An East Syriac liturgical page does not automatically document the pronunciation of a present-day Sureth speaker, and a manuscript style cannot be used as a simple label for a modern ethnic identity."),
    ("Images are not automatically reusable", "HMML's teaching pages include attributed manuscript photographs with rights retained by custodians. This archive links directly to their annotated examples rather than copying their images. For any future manuscript comparison, identify the holding institution, shelfmark, folio, reproduction credit, typeface or hand, and licence. Modern letterform illustrations should be clearly distinguished from historical reproductions.")
], ["unicode-syriac-standard","unicode-syriac-names","hmml-estrangela","hmml-serto","hmml-east","hmml-palaeography"], ["syriac-scripts","classical-syriac","syriac-unicode-reading","manuscripts"])

add("syriac-unicode-reading", "Syriac letters, direction and digital text", "Language & literature",
    "A source-anchored primer for identifying Syriac Unicode letters without inventing pronunciations or manuscript forms.", [
    ("A character is not its drawing", "Unicode names identify characters such as ALAPH (U+0710, ܐ), BETH (U+0712, ܒ) and TAW (U+072C, ܬ). The same code point may appear in very different letterforms under Estrangela, Serto or East Syriac styling. The language hub prints the twenty-two core letter identities from the Unicode name list. Its shapes depend on fonts installed on the reader's device; no claimed historical hand is reproduced."),
    ("Right-to-left writing and connected forms", "The Unicode Standard specifies that Syriac runs from right to left and follows contextual joining rules. Correct text shaping requires a suitable font and bidirectional implementation. A Syriac word copied as a sequence of valid code points may display incorrectly in software without Syriac support. Screenshots should not be preferred over editable text when preserving records, but both the text and font environment are worth documenting."),
    ("Letters are not a complete pronunciation key", "Unicode character names are encoding identifiers, not a phonetic transcription standard or an audio lesson. Vowel and other diacritical marks have separate code points; traditions vary in their use and historical forms. Reading a modern spoken language from an unpointed manuscript needs linguistic training, local convention and context. This introduction deliberately does not assign one modern pronunciation to each character."),
    ("Safely cite digital examples", "When presenting a Syriac sample, identify whether it comes from a specific manuscript, published edition, contemporary writer or a simple encoding illustration. Quote only what rights allow, and record any normalisation, added vocalisation or editorial transliteration. Text whose source cannot be checked should not be published as an authenticated biblical original or a supposedly ancient inscription.")
], ["unicode-syriac-names","unicode-syriac-standard","hmml-palaeography"], ["syriac-script-histories","syriac-scripts","manuscripts","classical-syriac"])

add("nena-dialect-comparison", "Listening across North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic dialects", "Language & literature",
    "Why Urmi, Alqosh and Ankawa belong to distinct documented speech communities rather than one generic accent.", [
    ("The dialect database is not a single voice", "Cambridge's North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic project organises descriptions, transcriptions and audio by community and locality. Christian and Jewish speech traditions, including some now endangered or no longer spoken, are represented. The database makes it possible to compare particular recordings, but its available samples are uneven across dialects. A recorded pronunciation is evidence for its speaker and locality, not an automatic pronunciation rule for all Assyrians."),
    ("Alqosh in its own record", "Cambridge catalogues the Alqosh Christian dialect separately and credits linguistic work associated with Eleanor Coghill. The site links to recorded narratives and grammatical descriptions. These materials are valuable because their spoken context is preserved alongside transcript or linguistic explanation. Anyone borrowing a word or phrase should note the local variety, the recording or text identifier and the scholarly transcription convention."),
    ("Ankawa in its own record", "The Cambridge Ankawa record is identified as Christian, from north-eastern Iraq, and credits work by Roberta Borghero. It includes a locality-labelled oral narrative. Compare the same grammatical or lexical feature with Alqosh only where each record actually contains comparable evidence. A difference may reflect morphology, local usage, speaker background or transcription method, rather than an error to 'fix' by imposing one spelling."),
    ("A research-led comparison", "Choose one research question, such as a particular pronoun form or verb construction, and document both the source variety and the fieldwork context. Distinguish heard speech, phonetic or phonological transcription, Syriac-script rendering and English translation. The archive links to audio hosted by Cambridge and does not redistribute it or train a synthetic universal voice. A future approved comparison should involve qualified speakers from the documented communities.")
], ["cambridge-nena","nena-dialects","nena-alqosh","nena-ankawa"], ["sureth","urmi-neo-aramaic-recordings","endangered-languages","oral-history"])

add("urmi-neo-aramaic-recordings", "Urmi: recording a living Neo-Aramaic variety", "Language & literature",
    "Fieldwork-based listening and language history around the Urmia region and its diasporas.", [
    ("Local speech, not one national norm", "The University of Cambridge lists Christian Urmi as a distinct documented North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic variety, with fieldwork credited to Geoffrey Khan. The record includes audio and transcripts. That is a meaningful linguistic label, not proof that every family from Urmia, every Assyrian speaker or every church used exactly the same pronunciation. Migration, schooling and literary standards add further layers."),
    ("A documented oral archive", "The Cambridge Christian Urmi page provides topics such as village life and other narratives. These recordings are linked as evidence of individual storytelling and language use. The site's grammatical feature counts describe entries in a database, not the total complexity or the importance of a dialect. No recording or transcript is copied into this archive without documented permission."),
    ("Urmia and print", "Historical studies of Urmia describe printing and publication in Syriac and local Neo-Aramaic varieties. Printed standards, liturgical Classical Syriac and vernacular speech have overlapping but distinct roles. A particular press or newspaper cannot be assumed to represent every speaker's home language. Where dates, titles or scripts are discussed, consult the documented printing history rather than treating one printed spelling as universally normative."),
    ("What a reliable comparison needs", "A future lesson could place a short, permission-cleared Urmi recording beside a speaker-approved transcript, literal gloss and optional English translation. It would also name the speaker's locality, consent terms, transcription conventions and date. The archive currently links to institutional recordings as the more reliable option; it makes no claim to supply a reviewed pronunciation course.")
], ["nena-urmi","cambridge-nena","gedsh-urmia","gedsh-printing"], ["sureth","nena-dialect-comparison","syriac-printing","urmia"])

add("surayt-documentation", "Surayt and Turoyo: learning and documentation", "Language & literature",
    "A careful entryway into the language of Tur Abdin and its diaspora, distinct from North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic.", [
    ("A separate contemporary language tradition", "The GEDSH Turoyo entry and scholarship on the Tur Abdin region describe a modern Aramaic language also called Surayt. It is not simply an accent of Christian Urmi or Alqosh speech. The relationship among local speech, ecclesiastical Classical Syriac and written materials is historical and sociolinguistic; speakers' self-designations should be recorded in their own terms."),
    ("A course with an identified authoring context", "Freie Universitat Berlin announced an advanced SLOMO SURAYT online course containing structured grammar and texts. This is a credible route into material prepared for this language variety. The archive can link to the university's learning pages without reproducing their lessons, audio, vocabulary lists or illustrations. A learner should confirm which level and spelling practices a lesson assumes."),
    ("Writing is a separate editorial decision", "Modern Surayt has written practices that vary across publications and learning projects. A script choice is not evidence of a community's sole authentic identity, and Classical Syriac spelling cannot simply be transferred to conversational Surayt without explanation. Future examples require labelled orthography, a named linguistic source and approval from competent speakers before being presented as instructional material."),
    ("Place and diaspora context", "Tur Abdin has a documented linguistic and monastic heritage, while diaspora transmission occurs in different educational and family settings. Endangerment and preservation questions involve people, institutions and intergenerational transmission, not just digitising a font. A useful archive entry preserves local name, date, institution and consent, and avoids using one course to define all speakers.")
], ["gedsh-turoyo","gedsh-tur-abdin","fu-surayt","unicode-syriac-standard"], ["surayt","tur-abdin","aramaic","endangered-languages"])

add("manuscript-provenance-practice", "How to document a Syriac manuscript", "Language & literature",
    "From a manuscript shelfmark to a reproducible historical citation with transparent rights and dating.", [
    ("Separate a work from its surviving copy", "A manuscript copied in a later century can preserve a much earlier composition. Record the physical manuscript's date or proposed date separately from an author's period and from any later binding or catalogue description. Institutional catalogues at Manchester and HMML are the starting point for this distinction. A copy date should not be silently presented as the date a text was composed."),
    ("A reproducible reference", "A useful manuscript citation identifies the institution, holding collection, shelfmark, folio or page, language, script, catalogue record and consultation date. If an image is cited, record the image provider and licensing statement as well. HMML's palaeography examples add observed letterform characteristics to those catalogue coordinates. A visual feature can support a comparison without providing proof of every proposed date."),
    ("Mixed hands and changing letterforms", "HMML's Serto and East Syriac lessons explicitly describe blended or ambiguous letter features in some manuscripts. Classifying a manuscript by one decorative heading may ignore the rest of the hand. A study should examine particular folios and marks; state when the palaeographic attribution is approximate; and avoid claiming that every Syriac-looking manuscript came from one ethnic group or place."),
    ("Access and reuse are different permissions", "A digitised collection may make images available for reading without authorising redistribution or modification. The syri.ac discovery directory and institutional collections point readers to the custodians who can clarify conditions. The archive should preserve catalogue links and original scholarly credits, seek explicit permission for new facsimiles, and treat family papers or oral history as requiring additional consent beyond copyright.")
], ["hmml-palaeography","hmml-serto","hmml-east","hmml-voobus","manchester-syriac","syriac-digimss"], ["manuscripts","syriac-script-histories","research-guide","heritage-stewardship"])

add("living-language-archive-ethics", "Preserving living Assyrian language with consent", "Language & literature",
    "Language heritage documentation that protects speakers, dialect specificity and future re-use rights.", [
    ("Documentation starts with people", "A living-language project should ask speakers how they identify their community, language and home variety. Cambridge's NENA work shows why such distinctions matter: its records span many Christian and Jewish local dialects with distinct linguistic histories. A file called 'Assyrian voice' without locality, date or speaker permissions is not an adequate scholarly record."),
    ("Record what the speaker authorised", "Written consent should specify whether an interview may be streamed, downloaded, transcribed, translated, quoted, used for teaching or included in research datasets. Those permissions are distinct, and people may have good reasons to restrict a family story, voice or image. Avoid creating public synthetic voice models from recordings or treating public listening access as unrestricted redistribution rights."),
    ("Preserve context, not just sound", "For a recording, retain a stable identifier, approximate date, language variety, locality where consent permits, recording environment, interviewer, transcription or translation credits and any redactions. The Cambridge databases illustrate how descriptive metadata helps listeners interpret variation. A future public archive should distinguish original recording, edited clip, transcription and editorial gloss as separate artefacts."),
    ("Enable correction and future review", "Transcriptions and transliterations can contain errors or differ under valid conventions. Speakers, descendants and qualified linguists should have a clear way to request corrections, contextual limits or withdrawal where terms allow. A responsible archive keeps an internal revision trail, credits named reviewers when consented to, and never marks material as verified just because it has been uploaded. Current submissions in this project remain download-only.")
], ["cambridge-nena","nena-dialects","modern-nena","modern-kanna"], ["endangered-languages","oral-history","nena-dialect-comparison","diaspora"])



# Milestone 5: original heritage interpretation with an explicit public-versus-private distinction.
add("oral-history-interview-method", "Interviewing Assyrian families and elders", "People & diaspora",
    "Preparing a respectful oral history, documenting its context and protecting the narrator's right to decide what happens next.", [
    ("Begin with a conversation, not a recording", "An oral-history project starts by explaining the purpose of the interview, intended listeners, the anticipated archive and the limits of control after release. Oral History Australia advises informed consent before recording and an opportunity for the interviewee to review, correct or withdraw material. Explain those choices in a language the narrator understands. A participant should be free to decline any question or stop the interview. Recording is not permission to place a file online."),
    ("Invite the ordinary details", "A family history can be richer when it includes everyday life: a home or village, school, agricultural work, trades, festivals, language at home, journeys and objects carried across borders. Questions should be open-ended rather than seeking confirmation of a pre-written historical narrative. People may identify themselves through different community, church or linguistic names. Record their own wording, and do not assign an ethnic or religious category based solely on a surname or place."),
    ("Keep testimony and historical claims legible", "An interview documents the narrator's remembered experience and interpretation. It can contain specific dates, family names or explanations later compared with contemporary records, without reducing the person's account to a pass-or-fail fact test. A migration document, dated photograph or institutional file may support part of a recollection, but it does not validate every statement. Oral history should be identified as attributed testimony, while a scholarly reconstruction should carry separate references."),
    ("Before an interview is shared", "Describe the intended access levels, who may quote or translate, the narrator's chosen public display name, any passages to restrict and how later corrections will be recorded. Oral History Australia recommends making those agreements clear and in writing. Include a review step for transcripts and translations; distinguish private preservation from public distribution. The Living Archive currently offers planning templates only; it does not receive, host or review private interviews.")
], ["oha-australia-ethics","oha-australia-practice","nla-oral-history-rights","modern-kanna"], ["oral-history","australia","living-language-archive-ethics","heritage-consent-and-access"])

add("assyrian-village-histories", "Researching Assyrian villages and place names", "Places & heritage",
    "A method for building village entries with dated maps, variant names and community testimony instead of one unqualified place label.", [
    ("Start with the place and the date", "Village history should distinguish a settlement, wider district, ecclesiastical jurisdiction and modern administrative name. The Nineveh Plain and Tur Abdin are different geographical and historical settings, and the full ancient Assyrian Empire is not a substitute for either. Record a place name as it appears in a dated source; preserve alternative spellings in Syriac, Arabic, Kurdish, Turkish or another language where the source documents them. Do not silently present one modern form as the original name for every century."),
    ("Use more than a family memory", "A careful village entry may combine archived photographs, land records, maps, school or church documents, oral histories and academic descriptions. Each type has limits. A family account can identify a household's experience, while a government record may document administrative decisions but omit community perspectives. An undated map is not evidence for historical population size, and a location marked on a modern map does not establish an ancient province. Note when the source is a first-party statement or an institutional report."),
    ("The Nineveh Plain as an example", "The archive's existing Nineveh Plain entry links to a dated United Nations account of meetings with local Christian and Shabak communities in Bartella and Hamdaniya in October 2018. This is evidence of discussions and concerns reported at that time, not a present-day census or a definitive list of village identities. Future town pages should document locality, variant names and dated access conditions; avoid projecting 2018 security information into the present."),
    ("Privacy and surviving places", "Some village histories involve conflict, displacement, missing relatives or contested property. A contributor may wish to describe cultural life without making their relatives or property identifiable to strangers. An editorial record should not publish exact household coordinates, family documents or testimony about third parties without assessing consent and possible harm. A public bibliography can point readers to archives while leaving sensitive material uncollected.")
], ["unami-nineveh","gedsh-tur-abdin","gedsh-sureth","oha-australia-ethics"], ["nineveh-plain","tur-abdin","urmia","oral-history-interview-method","heritage-consent-and-access"])

add("migration-memory-research", "Migration memories in the Assyrian diaspora", "People & diaspora",
    "Reading individual journeys alongside community institutions while keeping geography, date, language and recollection distinct.", [
    ("One account can be well documented", "The NSW Migration Heritage Centre's published Sophia Kanna account records an Assyrian migration experience, including arrival in Sydney in 1964. Its value is its named first-person perspective and institutional description, not an implied claim to represent every migrant. A responsible article credits the person and collection; it does not copy full testimony, assume all photographs are freely reusable or infer a total population from a single family's story."),
    ("Institutions preserve another perspective", "The Assyrian Australian Association dates its establishment to 1969 and its Resource Centre to 1984. These first-party institutional histories help identify organised settlement and cultural support in the Sydney region. Organisation websites are different evidence from private recollections, and their programme descriptions can change. When combining an association's chronology with a family story, identify which statement comes from which source."),
    ("A diaspora has multiple routes", "Scholarly summaries describe movements involving Urmia and communities in Europe, North America and Australia over different periods. The causes, opportunities and consequences varied, and not every household shared the same journey. Research should record departure locality, intermediate countries, destination and approximate years separately. Avoid converting an individual story into a claim that the diaspora began at one event or that all descendants maintain identical linguistic or ecclesiastical practices."),
    ("Build a comparable but humane record", "A voluntary record can use broad date ranges, places with consent, languages the narrator chooses to identify and links to approved documents. Ask what the narrator wishes to preserve, and allow anonymous or restricted versions where appropriate. If accounts disagree, make the differences visible rather than editing them into a false consensus. The archive's public examples remain external references, not newly collected personal records.")
], ["modern-kanna","modern-aaa","modern-arc","gedsh-diaspora","nla-oral-history-access"], ["diaspora","australia","oral-history","assyrian-australian-association","heritage-consent-and-access"])

add("heritage-objects-and-practices", "Songs, recipes, photographs and inherited objects", "Arts & traditions",
    "How to document Assyrian cultural practice without assuming one family's custom is universal or that a public recording is free to reuse.", [
    ("Context makes an object meaningful", "A family photograph or inherited object may carry a place, name, date, maker, occasion or journey. Record those fields separately and mark estimates clearly. A scan preserves a likeness, not the entire story of ownership or use. If a portrait includes living people or a child, assess privacy independently of copyright ownership. Public release can reveal family relationships or locations; preservation and publication therefore require different decisions."),
    ("Recipes are histories of variation", "SBS Food's attributed account of Evan Yako's kileche provides a particular recipe and cultural context. It should be described as one person's published version rather than a single authoritative preparation shared by all Assyrian families. A future community contribution might record ingredients, occasions, dialect names and changes after migration with the cook's permission. This archive links to the broadcaster instead of republishing its full protected recipe or photographs."),
    ("Performance needs named context", "Community presentations of khigga and Akitu celebrations can document an occasion, choreographers or performers, a group and the audience. The Assyrian Student Association of Arizona describes one dance crew; SBS Assyrian reports a particular Sydney New Year celebration. Such records help show living adaptation. They should not be treated as proof that a particular choreography has been performed unchanged since antiquity."),
    ("Record rights for every medium", "A music file, photograph, oral interview, handwritten poem and recipe may involve different contributors and rights. Permission to quote a speaker does not automatically clear background music or the rights of other performers. An editor should record creator, recording date, licence or agreement, public access restrictions, permitted extracts and corrections. Original recordings are not uploaded to this archive; users are directed to the source custodian.")
], ["modern-kileche","modern-dance","modern-festival-sydney","nla-oral-history-rights"], ["kileche","dance","akitu","music","heritage-consent-and-access"])

add("heritage-consent-and-access", "Consent, access and rights for heritage material", "People & diaspora",
    "A practical distinction between documenting a story privately, making it available for research and authorising public reuse.", [
    ("An interview agreement is specific", "Oral History Australia advises explaining recording, custody, copyright and potential later uses, and making a clear agreement before recording. Asking a person to participate is different from obtaining permission to publish their name, voice, image or an edited transcript. A suitable arrangement identifies participants and any other relevant rights holders, what material is covered, the intended repository and who can authorise future uses. The blank project checklist is a planning aid, not a signed release or legal opinion."),
    ("Research access is not public reuse", "The National Library of Australia explains that oral-history material may be open for research yet require written approval for personal copies or publication. Restrictions can differ for the audio, transcript and summary. Publicly accessible catalogue metadata therefore does not mean the interview can be re-hosted, transcribed or used in a training dataset. The archive may link to the custodian's authorised page while respecting its item-specific conditions."),
    ("Protect living people and third parties", "An interview may reveal personal addresses, health information, trauma, family disputes or the names of people who did not consent to participate. Contributors should not place such details in a public repository or browser-hosted demonstration. Separate the public descriptive record from restricted originals, document redactions and give the narrator a review opportunity where possible. Minors and vulnerable participants need appropriately designed safeguards and lawful permissions, not a generic checkbox."),
    ("Corrections, withdrawal and limits", "A preservation plan should identify an editor or custodian to receive correction or access requests, explain restrictions that may already have arisen through third-party publication, and document what can and cannot be withdrawn. Oral History Australia's guidance supports review, correction and withdrawal opportunities; an archive must not promise global erasure of copies already lawfully distributed. This project's contact and review workflow is not operational: downloading a draft does not send a request, establish consent or trigger publication.")
], ["oha-australia-ethics","oha-australia-practice","nla-oral-history-rights","nla-oral-history-access"], ["oral-history-interview-method","living-language-archive-ethics","heritage-stewardship","oral-history"])

add("assyria-community-broadcasts", "Community voices on radio and local media", "Arts & traditions",
    "Publicly catalogued broadcasting can document language, festivals and cultural adaptation while retaining the publisher's rights.", [
    ("Media as a dated historical witness", "SBS Assyrian provides published news, radio and podcast material, including interviews and event coverage. A particular broadcast can attest what was reported or said on that date. Its transcript, audio and images remain the publisher's material unless explicit reuse terms permit otherwise. A link to a publicly streamed programme should not be misrepresented as a recording commissioned or owned by this archive."),
    ("Examples from living culture", "SBS's report on Assyrian New Year celebrations in Sydney provides a dated example of community observance. An attributed SBS Food recipe and an Arizona student association's dance documentation show other ways cultural practice is presented publicly. None is a complete survey of Assyrian customs, and later researchers should identify event, speaker, location and date before drawing broad conclusions about continuity or change."),
    ("Language and reach", "Community broadcasters can connect diasporic listeners through shared language and current affairs. A broadcast may use a particular pronunciation, spelling or audience designation. It is evidence for that particular production, not an authoritative national standard for every Assyrian speaker. For dialect-specific linguistic comparison, the Cambridge North-Eastern Neo-Aramaic database provides a different type of source with academic fieldwork methods."),
    ("Preserve citations, not illicit copies", "A practical broadcast reference should retain the programme title, publisher, available publication date, stable URL, episode description, language and the moment or speaker being discussed. If an item later disappears online, document the broken link and an authorised alternative where one exists; do not bypass technical protection or mirror audio without rights. The Living Archive currently preserves external links and original editorial summaries only.")
], ["modern-radio","modern-festival-sydney","modern-kileche","modern-dance","cambridge-nena"], ["music","akitu","dance","heritage-objects-and-practices","living-language-archive-ethics"])


